{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>I am Esau your firstborn.</b> And likewise [when he said], 'Are you this my son Esau?' and he said, 'I' — there is no doubt that all of Jacob's words contain nothing but truth, and so the prophet testifies of him (Micah 7:20): ״תתן אמת ליעקב״, and this is what he said (Genesis 28:22): ״וכל אשר תתן לי עשר אעשרנו לך״ — and he had twelve sons, and Ephraim and Manasseh, about whom he said, 'they shall be to me as Reuben and Simeon,' so that they are fourteen; remove from them four firstborns for the four mothers, since the consecrated does not remove the consecrated, there remain ten, and the tribe of Levi is the tenth, which the Holy One, blessed be He, set apart to minister before Him and to bless in His name — see how his words were fulfilled. Also here, when he said ״אנכי עשו בכורך״, he intended by it: I am in the place of Esau your firstborn — that is, for the firstborn-ship, and for the sake of the firstborn-ship Isaac intended to bless Esau; therefore the blessings were fitting for Jacob in his place, since he had already taken the firstborn-ship from him, and he came to the firstborn-ship by his [Esau's] own act, and to all that is fitting for it, and one who comes by the act of his fellow — he is like him and stands in his place. For behold, the angel, when he spoke with the prophet, would say ״אני ה'״, and likewise the prophet when he spoke with Israel, and we find explicitly that the angel said to Jacob ״אנכי האל בית אל״, for he spoke in the language of his sender. From this Jacob said ״אנכי עשו בכורך״, for had he said 'I am Esau' and fallen silent, his statement would have been untrue, and because of this he added 'your firstborn,' for by reason of the firstborn-ship he comes in the place of Esau. And likewise [to] 'Are you this my son Esau' — that is, whom I have to bless, and he said 'I' — he did not say 'I am Esau,' but 'I,' that is: I am the one whom you are fitting to bless. And what he said, ״עשיתי כאשר דברת אלי״ — and he [Isaac] had spoken only to Esau — the word 'אלי' means 'for my sake' and 'for my benefit,' for had he not commanded Esau, Rebekah would not have heard and would not have commanded Jacob. And what he said, ״ואכלה מצידי״ — and he did not hunt at all — the word 'מצידי' means 'from my provision' that I bring to you, as in the verse (Joshua 9:14): ״ויקחו האנשים מצידם״, which means 'from their provision.'" "<b>Please arise, sit.</b> But Esau said, 'Let my father arise.' They said in the Midrash: Jacob spoke in language of honor, in language of supplication — ״קום נא שבה״; the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him his reward in that very language, for so Moses, who is of his seed, said (Numbers 10): ״קומה ה'״, ״שובה ה'״. But Esau spoke in language of arrogance — 'Let my father arise and eat' — and the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him his punishment in that very language itself, as it is written (Psalms 68:2): ״יקום אלהים יפוצו אויביו״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:42.694908309Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}